{-
   Storage that is not this function's to give away.

   # resolver: ownership

   A separate file from `Reject.Moves.yana` for a mechanical reason rather than a thematic one:
   these are reported while the body is being built, and resolution stops before the ownership
   analysis runs - so a single file could only ever show one of the two sets.

   The rule is `readMode`'s third answer read from the position's side. A place this frame does not
   own has nothing to hand over whatever the position asked for, so a read of one produces a *loan*;
   and a loan filling a `->` parameter is a value the callee consumes and the real owner releases
   again. Neither half is wrong on its own, which is exactly why the pair has to be caught where the
   position is still known - see `Resolver::readPlace`. Before it existed, `fn f(g: Guard) = eat(g)`
   compiled, and the guard was released twice.
-}

let &trace = 0 :: I32

data Guard {id: I32}

instance Drop(Guard):
    fn drop(->value: Guard):
        trace = trace + value.id

fn guard(id: I32) -> Guard = Guard {id: id}

fn eat(->g: Guard) -> I32 = g.id

data Pair {left: Guard, right: Guard}

-- A shared parameter is the caller's value. The callee may look at it and may not give it away.
fn fromBorrow(g: Guard) -> I32 = eat(g)

-- A field of one is the caller's too: reaching into storage does not change whose it is, which is
-- the rule `Builder::fieldPlace` states once and this is the visible half of.
fn fromBorrowedField(p: Pair) -> I32 = eat(p.left)

-- Two positions, one rule. A `return` is a sink exactly as a `->` argument is, so handing back a
-- borrowed value is the same mistake written differently.
fn returnBorrow(g: Guard) -> Guard = g

fn main() -> I32 = 0
